Butterflies undergo **complete metamorphosis**: egg, larva (caterpillar), pupa (chrysalis), then adult. The caterpillar mainly eats and grows; inside the chrysalis, its body is reorganized into the adult form.

Their wing scales are tiny, overlapping structures made mostly of **chitin**, a tough natural material also found in insect exoskeletons. In many blue species, microscopic ridges and layers reflect specific wavelengths of light, creating structural blue rather than relying on blue pigment.

No—this does not make them reptiles. Butterflies are **insects**, while reptiles are vertebrates with backbones. As for longevity, most adult butterflies live from several days to a few weeks, though some overwintering species can survive for many months. The lifespan record depends on whether one counts only adulthood or the entire life cycle; certain migratory or overwintering individuals are documented at roughly a year, sometimes a little longer.
